---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Explore Stocks Through Data & Rules-Based Analysis";
const description =
  "A free technical screener and reference for Indian equity markets. Filter NSE " +
  "stocks by RSI, ADX, moving averages and volume using thresholds you set, and " +
  "learn what each indicator actually measures.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Introduction">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site computes standard technical indicators across roughly 2,250
    NSE-listed stocks every trading day, and lets you filter them using
    conditions <strong>you</strong> define. It also explains what those
    indicators actually measure, and where they mislead.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It is a measurement and reference tool. It is not a tip service, it does not
    tell you what to buy, and it has no view on any individual stock.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea in one paragraph</h2>
  <p>
    Most screeners hand you somebody else's filter and show you the survivors.
    That hides the part that matters — how much the answer depends on where the
    thresholds were drawn. Here every threshold is a slider. Set RSI between 35
    and 70 and a few hundred stocks qualify; narrow it to 50–60 and the list
    collapses to a handful. Same data, same day, wildly different "results".
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Watching that happen teaches something no article can: a screen that looks
    selective is often just a threshold chosen after the fact.
  </p>

  <h2>What the data is</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>universe</code><span class="meta">~2,250 NSE-listed stocks scanned</span></div>
      <p>The most liquid 200 by 60-day median turnover are published. Thinly
        traded stocks are excluded deliberately — their indicator values are
        noisy and you could not trade them at the displayed price anyway.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>indicators</code><span class="meta">standard published formulas</span></div>
      <p>RSI(14), ADX(14), EMA 21/50/200, ATR, volume against its 20-day average,
        and distance from the 52-week high. Nothing proprietary — you can verify
        every one of them anywhere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>frequency</code><span class="meta">once per trading day</span></div>
      <p>Recomputed after the close. Every row carries the date of the price bar
        it came from, so anything stale is visible rather than passed off as
        current.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>missing_values</code><span class="meta">shown, never filled in</span></div>
      <p>Where a value cannot be computed it displays as unavailable. Nothing is
        estimated, interpolated or invented.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Start here</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/screener/">Screener</a></h3>
      <p>The main tool. Move the thresholds and watch the match count respond.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/learn/rsi/">Learn the indicators</a></h3>
      <p>What RSI, ADX, moving averages and volume measure — and what they don't.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/">Calculators</a></h3>
      <p>SIP, CAGR, XIRR, inflation, position sizing. All run in your browser.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>Beyond charts</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Technical indicators describe price behaviour. They say nothing about whether
    a business is any good. The <a href="/learn/reading-financials/">fundamentals
    section</a> covers the other half — reading a financial statement, ROE and
    ROCE, debt, the difference between profit and cash flow, and how to form a
    view on a business rather than a chart.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>no_buy_sell_calls</code><span class="meta">by design</span></div>
      <p>No entry prices, no stop-losses, no targets. A stock appearing in a
        filtered list means only that its values matched the thresholds you set.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>no_predictions</code><span class="meta">by design</span></div>
      <p>Every indicator here is a function of past prices. None of them forecast
        anything, and nothing on this site claims otherwise.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>no_personalised_advice</code><span class="meta">regulatory</span></div>
      <p>Whether any security suits you depends on your goals, horizon and risk
        tolerance — none of which this site knows. For personalised advice,
        consult a SEBI-registered investment adviser.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Built and maintained by <a href="/about/">Tilak Chandra Shastri</a>, an
    AMFI-registered mutual fund distributor (ARN-346031). The analytics behind
    this site — the data pipeline, the indicator calculations, the backtesting
    engine — started as a personal research project. This is the public part of
    it. Funded by advertising; see <a href="/about/">about</a> for how that works
    and what it does not influence.
  </p>
</Base>
